Actually, let me also double check the fix handles a subtle case: when the combined query already has `values_select` set from its own `.values()` call, we don't clone and don't re-apply — preserving behavior. And when the combined query is shared (same object appears twice), the first clone+set doesn't affect the second because each iteration clones independently from the ORIGINAL (which still has empty values_select). Good.

One more consideration: In `get_combinator_sql`, we iterate `compilers`. For `qs1.union(qs1)`, both compilers reference the SAME query object (`qs1.query`). First iteration: `not compiler.query.values_select` true → clone qs1.query (clone_a) → set_values on clone_a. Second iteration: `compiler.query` still references original qs1.query (empty values_select) → clone again (clone_b) → set_values. Both clones get the values independently. Before my fix, the second iteration would have seen the original already mutated. Now both work correctly.

