Now I can see the issue clearly. For many-to-many relations (which are multi-valued and may spawn duplicates), multiple `.filter()` calls create multiple duplicate joins (one per search term), while a single `.filter()` call with all Q objects combined reuses the same join.

This is exactly the bug described. So the fix is to build all Q objects and pass them to a single `queryset.filter(...)` call instead of looping with `queryset = queryset.filter(or_queries)`.

