[content]
The fix is complete. I overrode `CommandParser.add_subparsers()` so that when subparsers are created, the `called_from_command_line` attribute is propagated to each constructed subparser (via `functools.partial` on the parser class). This ensures:

1. When a management command is invoked from the command line (`called_from_command_line=True`), missing arguments inside subparsers produce a proper human-facing usage message and `SystemExit(2)` instead of an uncaught `CommandError` stack trace.
2. Programmatic usage (`called_from_command_line` falsy) still raises `CommandError` as before.
3. Explicitly-passed custom `parser_class` subclasses of `CommandParser` are preserved and also get the flag propagated, while non-`CommandParser` classes (e.g. plain `ArgumentParser`) are left untouched.

All 44 existing `user_commands` tests pass and the reproduction from the issue now shows the friendly usage message

