Wait, but `RemoveIndex.reduce(AddIndex)` - if RemoveIndex is followed by AddIndex with the same index name, then the net effect is... RemoveIndex removes the index, then AddIndex adds it back. That could be optimized to either nothing (if the index object is the same) or to a "replace" operation. But since we can't know if the re-added index is the same as the removed one (RemoveIndex only stores the name), this is ambiguous. Actually, in the autodetector scenario, if you have RemoveIndex followed by AddIndex with the same name but potentially different fields, the net effect is "alter index". But we can't replace it with nothing because the fields might differ. So the safe optimization is to NOT cancel them.

